## Validation

Primary validation:

- nested stratified cross-validation;
- balanced accuracy and macro-F1;
- class-wise sensitivity/specificity;
- log loss, Brier score, and calibration curve;
- bootstrap confidence intervals for all metrics.

Leakage checks:

- fit replacement, closure, ILR basis, scaling, and feature selection only on training folds;
- group splits if multiple measurements belong to one artifact;
- do not use reference labels, post hoc class descriptions, or test-set weathering information during training.

## Sensitivity and Robustness

Repeat the analysis over:

- zero-replacement values \(\delta\in\{10^{-6},10^{-5},10^{-4}\}\);
- alternative ILR bases;
- raw versus normalized proportions as a diagnostic;
- class weighting versus unweighted loss;
- weathering strata, if available;
- bootstrap resamples and closure-preserving perturbations;
- supplementary validity thresholds, clearly separated from the prescribed `[85%,105%]` primary rule.

## Falsification

A credible model should:

- lose performance under label permutation;
- remain better than the majority baseline under repeated resampling;
- not depend on one component alone;
- show degradation under targeted closure-preserving perturbations;
- produce calibrated probabilities rather than only hard labels.

If label permutation retains comparable performance, leakage or duplicated records is suspected.

## Reviewer Risks

Main risks are automatic treatment of blanks as zeros, use of raw proportions without closure handling, invalid-row filtering after model fitting, data leakage through preprocessing, unsupported claims from small or imbalanced samples, and overinterpretation of weathered specimens. Exact sample-level conclusions remain pending until the XLSX is legitimately parsed in a permitted execution.

